# Fictional EP Tracklist

## Track 1: Rain Gauge Opens on the Third Strike

**Story & Context:** At 110 BPM, Adwoa opens with a three-hit gyil cell: low fifth, upper second, middle tonic. Julian leaves the first two beats almost empty. The third strike activates the full Gourd Gate: Kojo answers with a fretless sixth-to-root figure, Clara enters one eighth later with a major-6/9 FM voicing, and Mariam waits until the buzz tail thins before adding a muted trumpet third. The chorus repeats the same three pitches but uses Wake-like reinterpretation through different harmony: the final tonic now opens a minor-7 field. Yaw adds one diagonal shekere shake only after every second complete cell.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Close-up of a mechanical rain gauge beside three chalk marks on a weather ledger, a gyil mallet resting across the page, wet red-earth reflections outside a green-framed window. Sparse lyric fragments: “third strike,” “open the gauge,” “rain came late,” “write it down before it dries.”

## Track 2: Buzz Lasts Longer Than the Forecast

**Story & Context:** At 116 BPM, this is the EP’s tightest groove. Julian programs compact kick, dry snare, narrow clap, and straight sixteenth hats. Adwoa alternates two neighboring gyil keys with deliberately different decay lengths. Buzz Dividend becomes the hook: Clara’s FM chord stays short after the dry key but holds almost twice as long after the buzzier key. Kojo uses Fretless Counterfall, descending whenever the gyil cell climbs. During the middle section, three consecutive Mallet Vacancies remove snare, keyboard, and bass events one at a time, exposing the wooden strike as the only accent before the full groove returns.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Weather forecast sheet under a glass desk cover, one hand-drawn resonance line extending beyond the printed rainfall column, two gyil keys reflected in the glass, muted teal and cream palette. Sparse lyric fragments: “longer than forecast,” “still buzzing,” “leave the column open,” “the number changed after noon.”

## Track 3: Mica Sky After the Office Closes

**Story & Context:** The closer runs at 104 BPM and begins with only gyil, fretless bass, and FM electric piano. The drum machine enters after sixteen bars with kick and rim only. Adwoa uses widely spaced two-note cells, letting each gourd tail fully decay. Mariam’s muted trumpet becomes the principal Glass Answer voice, entering on soft sixths and falling half-step approaches near the end of each buzz. In the final minute, Julian removes the snare, then hats, then kick. Kojo sustains one low fifth while Clara holds a final add9 chord. Adwoa strikes one last wooden tonic, but no electric instrument follows it. The EP ends on the real gourd resonance and room tone.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Empty meteorological office after sunset, one green desk lamp still on, mica-grey sky beyond the veranda, gyil mallets resting beside a closed rainfall ledger, wet ground reflecting fluorescent light. Sparse lyric fragments: “office closed,” “mica sky,” “no answer after this,” “leave the last line blank.”

